  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, steeper along x, drawn at 0.3 × loss over x in [-2, 2], z in [-3.5, 3.5]. Each step: move = -learning rate × (true slope + noise). Along x the steps stay stable only below learning rate 2/3. Above that, swings grow.",
    "Noise is Gaussian, centred on the true slope, with spread 2·sqrt((64−b)/(63·b)) per direction for batch size b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at true length in bowl units (scale 1). Orange: the actual next move (mini-batch step). Grey: −learning rate × true slope. They are hidden once a run ends, and the arrow length then reads 0.",
    "Diverged: the loss went above 6, which is the edge of the drawn bowl. A run also ends after 40 steps. The trail holds the start point plus one point per step (at most 41). The ghost holds the last run.",
    "Noise comes from fixed seed 7 and is indexed by run and step. Reset replays the same sequence. A new run uses the next noise values. A diverged ball is drawn held at the bowl edge, while the loss readout keeps its true value."
  ]
}